<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Mihrabanid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1236 CE – 1537 CE (approx.) · capital Zaranj</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Persian dynasty of maliks that ruled Sistan for three centuries under Mongol, Timurid and Safavid overlords.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-din-ali-ibn-masud">Shams al-Din Ali ibn Mas'ud</a> established the dynasty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> in 1236, after the Mongol devastation ended the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nasrid-maliks-of-sistan">Nasrid Maliks of Sistan</a>. The family claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaranj">Zaranj</a> and later other centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmand-river">Helmand</a> delta.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Mihrabanid maliks governed an irrigated land of canals and dams on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmand-river">Helmand</a>, repeatedly damaged by war. They accepted the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kart-dynasty">Kart dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, and suffered heavily when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> in 1383, destroying dams and towns. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> they continued as local rulers, keeping the old title malik and Sistan's distinct identity. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ihya-al-muluk">Ihya al-Muluk</a>, a later local chronicle, records their history.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>; by 1537 the maliks had lost independence, although members of the family served as Safavid governors.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mihrabanid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Mihrabanid dynasty" width="320" height="221"><figcaption>Image: Arab_Hafez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mihrabanids_1236%E2%80%93Mid-16th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1236 CE – 1537 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zaranj</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shams al-Din Ali ibn Mas'ud</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, later Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iran, Afghanistan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mihrabanids, Maliks of Sistan</dd></dl></aside></div>

